// Delegatecall-reachable selfdestruct: a `SELFDESTRUCT` that executed
// successfully as the *direct child* of a `DELEGATECALL` frame — the
// structural shape of the 2017 Parity multisig wallet library incident,
// where a `SELFDESTRUCT` reachable only via `DELEGATECALL` from proxy
// wallets destroyed the shared library contract itself and froze every
// wallet depending on it.
//
// Why this is a distinct, higher-value signature than plain
// `unsafe_selfdestruct`: `DELEGATECALL` executes the callee's code
// against the *caller's* own storage and context
// (`fact_model::CallKind::executes_in_caller_context`) — but the
// `SELFDESTRUCT` opcode itself always destroys whichever contract's
// code is *currently executing*, i.e. the delegatecall's callee (the
// "library"), not the caller ("proxy") whose storage was being
// borrowed. A `SELFDESTRUCT` reached this way is therefore uniquely
// dangerous: destroying the library contract breaks *every* proxy that
// still delegates to it, not just the single caller in this one trace.
// A `SELFDESTRUCT` invoked directly (not through a delegatecall) only
// ever destroys the contract an attacker was already calling into
// directly — a strictly narrower blast radius. This pattern exists to
// surface that specific, worse shape as its own reviewable finding,
// alongside (not instead of) `unsafe_selfdestruct`.
//
// How this is expressed: `call(...)` gained a new optional
// `parent_kind` attribute this milestone (`crates/dsl/src/schema.rs`),
// mirroring `storage(call_kind: ...)`'s existing "dereference to the
// producing/invoking call, not mere trace-order adjacency" idiom —
// applied here to `fact_model::Call::parent`, the one-hop call-tree
// edge every `Call` already carries, instead of
// `StorageChange::call_id`. `call(kind: SelfDestruct, succeeded: true,
// parent_kind: Delegate)` is a single, self-contained evidence clause:
// it demands the SELFDESTRUCT's own direct parent frame — not merely
// some delegatecall elsewhere in the trace — be a DELEGATECALL (or its
// historical predecessor CALLCODE, which `call_kind_matches` already
// treats identically to a plain DELEGATECALL, per
// `CallKind::executes_in_caller_context`).
//
// No fact-model extension was needed: `Call::parent: Option<CallId>`
// already existed (every call tree needs it for depth/ancestry), so
// this milestone's only additive change was the DSL attribute and the
// matcher predicate filter that reads it — see
// `crates/matcher/src/predicate.rs`'s `evaluate_call` and its
// `call_predicate_parent_kind_*` tests.
//
// Scope note: like `unsafe_selfdestruct`, this pattern cannot verify
// whether the delegatecall itself was authorized, or whether the
// library's own SELFDESTRUCT function had (and lacked) an access
// modifier in source — the fact model has no access-control fact (see
// the top-level README's "Access-control failure" row). It flags the
// structural shape a reviewer needs surfaced, not a proof of missing
// access control.
pattern delegatecall_reachable_selfdestruct version 1 {
    family: DelegatecallReachableSelfdestruct
    severity: Critical
    tags: ["selfdestruct", "delegatecall", "access-control", "irrevocable", "library"]
    references: ["SWC-106", "SCWE-038"]

    evidence {
        required destruct_via_delegatecall: call(
            kind: SelfDestruct,
            succeeded: true,
            parent_kind: Delegate
        )
    }

    constraint: destruct_via_delegatecall
}
